T bhre cgls te cmoemnbdionuast cn onf is aelnlid anpcpe antid i fportmenatiaolns within the digital economy era. Among them, gas sensing technology represents one of the elegant technical means for information perception and acquisition, which have been extensively utilized in the detection of industrial gas leakage and food freshness, indoor/outdoor air quality monitoring, medical diagnosis, etc.1,2 Gas sensors, typically entailing sensing materials and transducers, can translate a variation in electric/ dielectric, thermometric, magnetic, optical, colorimetric, acoustic, and/or gravimetric properties into detectable signals under exposure to variable concentrations of gaseous analytes.3−7 The sensing materials typically interact with the targeted gas analytes to change their intrinsic physical and/or chemical parameters such as conductivity $(\sigma)$ , work function $(\varphi)$ , and permittivity $(\varepsilon)$ , which can be converted into a detectable signal including capacitance $(C)$ /impedance $(Z)$ , inductance $\bar{(L)}$ , resistance $(R)$ , current $(I)$ , and voltage $(V)$ by a transducer (Figure 1).8 Gas sensors can be mainly divided into chemiresistors, capacitors, field-effect transistors/Kelvin probes, electrochemical, quartz crystal microbalance, and optical sensors according to their conduction mechanism and/or transducing architecture.9−11 There are several well-established indexes used to evaluate the performance of gas sensors. First, sensitivity measures the ability of the sensors to detect gas analytes, and is often defined as the slope of the output characteristic curve.8,12 It is considered to be one of the most important performance indexes of sensors. Second, selectivity gauges the sensor’s ability to differentiate between certain gases and avoid interference from other coexisting gases in the gas system. It is expressed as the ratio of allowable coexisting components to the content of components to be measured under a specified measurement accuracy. The higher the ratio, the better the selectivity. Third, the limit of detection (LOD) is the minimum concentration or value of a gas analyte that can be detected from the sample with a specified precision and reproducibility. Fourthly, response/ recovery time measures the time the sensors take to respond to and recover from the targeted gases, typically defined as the time used for a $90\%$ change in signal. Finally, reproducibility and stability measure the ability of the sensor to produce the same output signal under different experimental conditions and within the required time, respectively.8,13 However, the above sensing performance is closely related to the structure and composition of the sensing materials. Therefore, it is essential to choose appropriate sensing materials based on the requirements of the actual application scenarios. Significant strides have been made toward the preparation of gas sensors based on various materials including metal oxide semiconductor s,14−16 conjugated polytmraenrs,ition mpeietazl ldeicthrailc cgernaimdeics,s2,3,24 lbaocrk  noistrpihdoe,r2u5 and MXenes.26,27 The recently reported devices based on those materials enable the detection of various gases with salient sensing properties such as relatively high sensitivity and stability, fast response/recovery, and low detection limit and power consumption.24,26,28 However, the fabrication of miniaturized gas sensors with high sensitivity and selectivity that is associated with the intrinsic properties (composition, porosity, conductivity, defect level, etc.) of sensing materials and the transducing mechanism is highly desirable but remains challenging. Therefore, there lies in an urgent need to develop sensing materials that exhibit super palpable changes in physicochemical properties under exposure to various gas analytes.29,30
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers (PCPs) represent a new type of crystalline porous materials constructed by organic ligands and inorganic metal nodes via coordination bonds.31,32 Their structural topology and porosity (aperture size and geometry) are designable by the judicious choices of inorganic second building units and/or organic ligands, as well as synthesis conditions.33−47 MOFs have been used in a wide range of applications including molecular storage ,/5s5,e5p6araantidocn,h4e8m−5o0/cbaitoasleysnisi,n51g,5s2ednrsuogrsd.5e7l vCeorym,5p3,a5r4eednewrigthy the commonly utilized sensing materials (e.g., polymers, metal oxide semiconductors (MOSs), carbon-based materials, and MXenes), MOFs featuring the merits of high porosity, large specific surface area, controllable pore size/geometry, potential functionalities, and tailor-made physicochemical properties, have been regarded as excellent sensing materials for targeted gas detection. Recently, great advances in the shaping of MOFs onto versatile substrates as functional films have laid the foundation for the preparation of MOF-based sensing devices including MOF-based chemiresistive sensors,58 capacitors,59 FETs,60 electrochemical sensors,61 QCMs,62 microresonators,63 and surface acoustic waves (SAWs).64 MOF coatings serve as either the sensing layers or molecule-sieving layers outside the active MOS structures. Both approaches take advantage of the highly porous architectures and the optional pore size of MOFs for sieving and preconcentrating gas molecules, thereby enabling high sensitivity and selectivity. Additionally, the intrinsic properties of the organic ligands and metal centers directly affect the surface acidity and redox activity of MOFs,65 thus affecting the selectivity, LOD, etc. of the MOF-coated sensors. MOFs, featuring high specific surface area, abundant active sites, and thus fast gas adsorption and transport, have a wide range of applications for detecting targeted harmful gases. Several decent reviews have summarized the material design and potential applications of MOF-coated devices for gas sensing from different aspects.25,26,29,49 This review summarizes the latest progress of MOF-coated gas sensors with emphases on their working principles and associated structure−property relationship. First, we review the advances of MOF-based gas sensing, including chemiresistive sensors, capacitors, FETs/KPs, electrochemical sensors, and QCM-based gas sensors. Within this regard, the structure and composition of MOFs are deliberately related to the observed sensing performance.65−67 We afterward present encouraging prospects and directions including major challenges and prospects about the long-time development and potentially practical application of MOF-based miniaturized sensing devices.
## INTEGRATION OF MOFS ONTO SENSING DEVICES
The inherent characteristics of sensing materials and their integration into sensing platforms are critical factors that directly influence the performance of the sensors. MOFs with relatively poor solution processability hamper their microfabrication and device integration onto various substrates, thereby restricting their application in sensing scenarios.68−70 Nonetheless, advances in MOF film preparation have been made, including methods such as electrochemical deposition,71 spin-coating and/or drop-castin g,72−74 direct hydro/solvothermal growth, $^{69,75,76}$ interfacial synthesis coupled with a subsequent transfer,77 template-mediated growth/transformation,69 and spray-coating.68,78 These approaches vary, of course, greatly in precision and control, but all contribute to overcoming the limitations of MOFs in device integration. Moreover, the 9−81 combination of new flexible substrates7 with MOFs has expanded MOFs’ application prospect in the fabrication of new intelligent wearable devices. Hereafter discusses some representative techniques suitable for the fabrication of MOF-coated sensing devices.
Spin-Coating and/or Drop-Casting. Spin-coating and/or drop-casting represents a simple and low-cost method for preparing MOF thin films, which are formed by depositing a MOF dispersion onto the desired substrates. The subsequent evaporation of the solvent affords MOF films with variable thicknesses depending on the MOF concentration, solution volume, and crystal size of MOFs. The two methods feature the obvious advantages of low cost, simple operation, and potential mass production of relevant MOF films irrespective of substrates.82 However, the requirement of ultrasonic treatment for forming homogeneous MOF suspension may induce structural defects and the clear boundaries between MOF crystals may worsen the charge/mass transport and thus the device performance.83
Direct Hydro/Solvothermal Growth. This technique involves the in situ synthesis and growth of MOF crystals onto variable substrates from solutions containing the building blocks for constructing MOFs, leading to the formation of either continuous and well-adhered MOF films or isolated MOF crystals. A series of MOF films such as MOF-5, $^{84,85}\mathrm{Ni-MOF},^{86}$ and $\mathbf{MIL-}160,^{87}$ etc. have been successfully grown onto various substrates including Au interdigitated electrodes (IDEs) and $\alpha$ - $\mathbf{Al}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ substrates.88 The direct growth approach offers obvious advantages for the MOF film fabrication. For instance, it allows for the precise control of the film thickness, morphology, and composition. Additionally, the direct growth approach can provide excellent adhesion between the MOF film and the substrate, leading to improved film stability. However, the approach typically needs delicate optimization of growth conditions, e.g., the concentration of precursors, the molar ratio between the metal ions and organic linkers, the temperature, and even the growth time. Additionally, substrates utilized for this method generally require subtitle treatments, including surface functionalization to facilitate the nucleation and growth of MOF crystals. This makes it more suitable for laboratory-scale preparation rather than large-scale industrial production of MOF films.69,75
Interfacial Synthesis Coupled with Subsequent Transfer. Interfacial synthesis coupled with subsequent transfer is a method that can form large-area MOF films on various substrates. The interfacial synthesis of free-standing MOF films can occur at liquid/liquid interfaces and/or gas/liquid interfaces. For the former, metal ions and organic ligands are dissolved respectively in two immiscible solvents, crystallizing at the interface to form free-standing MOF films with well-defined structures and properties through the diffusion of metal ions and organic ligands.77,89 The judicious choice of solvent depends largely on the MOF components and their solubilities in the respective phases. This approach enables fine control of film thickness and morphology by adjusting parameters such as reactant concentrations and reaction time. The resultant freestanding MOF films can be further transferred to variable flat substrates via Langmuir−Blodgett technique.90
Template-Mediated Growth/Transformation. Template-mediated growth and transformation usually involve two steps. The first step involves the direct preparation of intermediate layers, such as metals, metal hydroxides, metal oxides, and inorganic salts, to serve as templates and provide the metal sources required for constructing the desired MOFs.69 The next step concerns the formation of MOF films through the controllable dissolution of the preformed layers under a suitable hydro/solvothermal condition and further coordination with the organic ligands within solvents69 and/or even under vapors of organic linkers.91 Impressively, the solvent-free MOF-CVD (chemical vapor deposition) approach is compatible with the widely used photolithography during microfabrication and circumvents potential chemical contamination.91,92 For instance, Ameloot and his colleagues succeeded in preparing Cu-BHT ( $\mathrm{\BHT=}$ benzenehexanothiolate) thin films on $\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ substrate by solvent-free CVD method in their recent work.91 The process was achieved by the reaction of solid $\mathtt{C u O}$ precursor with the ligand vapor, and the resulting films were smooth and uniform with a thickness of $20{-}85~\mathrm{~nm}$ . The combination of CVD and lithography enabled high-fidelity patterning of MOF films, which encouraged the integration of MOF films onto micro/nano devices.
Spray-Coating. The spray-coating method involves the deposition of MOF precursors and/or MOF suspensions onto various substrates by spray nozzles or atomizers.78 This process can be further facilitated by applying heat or using a controlled environment. Typically, uniform and defect-free MOF films with controllable thicknesses can be obtained onto diverse substrates by adjusting parameters such as the spray pressure, nozzle size, and the distance between the spray gun and substrate.68 Overall speaking, the spray-coating technique provides a convenient and scalable approach for the fabrication of MOF films with potential applications in gas sensing.68,93
## CASE STUDIES OF MOF-BASED GAS SENSING
The sensing principles of MOF-based sensing devices rely on signal transduction stemming from the surface reaction and/or interaction between gas molecules and the sensing materials.8,13,33,63 The targeted analyte-MOF interactions cause changes in the physical properties of the sensing materials, and these changes can be detected by the transducer and translated into one or more measurable. Noteworthily, the interactions between the targeted analyte and materials typically involve noncovalent interactions94,95 (e.g., van der Waals forces, hydrogen bonding, and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions) and covalent bonding,94,96 which are mainly determined by the detected gas molecules and the structure and composition of the sensing material. MOFs featuring adjustable pore sizes/structure, abundant active sites, and host−guest interactions would undoubtedly facilitate the interaction. According to transduction schemes, MOF-based chemical devices for gas sensing can be divided into three main categories: optical, mass transducing, and electronic sensors.97 This review focuses only on electronic and mass transducing devices utilized for MOFbased gas sensing, which are capable of detecting the fluctuations of the electronic properties and mass of MOFs, e.g., resistance, capacitance, vibration frequencies or work function, and mass relying on the interaction with the targeted gases/vapors. Various MOFs have been utilized so far for gas sensing. For example, some (semi)conductive MOFs such as $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HITP}\big)_{2}^{\mathrm{\Delta98}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HHTP}\big)_{2}^{\mathrm{\scriptsize~99}}$ have been applied in chemiresistive sensing and a series of insulating MOFs (MOF74,10 UiO-66,100 and MIL-53,101 etc.) have been used for capacitive sensing, FETs and QCMs. Table 1 summarizes the representative MOF structures and their associated sensing performance of relevant MOF-coated devices.
## MOF-BASED CHEMIRESISTIVE SENSORS
The mechanism of chemiresistors (as shown in Figure 2a) relies mainly on the electron and hole transfer caused by the physical or chemical adsorption between gas analytes and sensing materials, or the reaction of gases on the sensing material, resulting in the change of the resistance (or conductivity) of the sensing material.96,102 The large specific surface area and abundant active sites of MOFs provide necessary conditions for their application in chemiresistive sensing. The application forms of MOFs in chemiresistive sensors mainly include the direct usage of MOFs as sensing materials and the coating of MOFs outside the surface of MOS sensors103,104 for filtering the interference components. In this review, we consider only the former.
Semiconductive MOF-Coated Chemiresistors. Since the overlap between the $p$ orbital of the organic linker and the $d$ orbital of the metal centers within the framework is low, the majority of MOFs thus exhibit low conductivity, limiting their application for chemiresistive gas sensors.105 MOFs with a twodimensional (2D) topology typically show modest conductivity owing to the $\pi$ -conjugated planar structure and charge delocalization. To date, a limited choices of semiconductive MOFs with modest conductivity have been reported.106−110 Zhang et al. fabricated, for the first time to the best of our knowledge, semiconductive ZIF-67 and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ (imidazole $(\mathrm{im}))_{2}$ coated chemiresistive sensors to detect formaldehyde and trimethylamine (TMA), respectively.111,112 The ZIF-67 coated sensors revealed good selectivity and a low detection limit (5 ppm) toward variable concentrations of formaldehyde. In addition, the $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{im})_{2}$ coated sensors showed better selectivity and higher responses toward TMA over other detected gases, such as methanol, formaldehyde, acetone, ammonia, and methane, ascribed to the weak interactions between $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{im})_{2}$ and TMA. Although these studies opened up avenues for the study of semiconductive MOF-based sensors, it is worth noting that these sensors operated at relatively high temperatures of 150 and $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In contrast, Dinca and his co-workers reported the application of 2D conductive MOFs for chemiresistive sensing at room temperature (RT).98,113 The $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HITP}\big)_{2}$ coated IDEs were utilized to detect $0.5\mathrm{\ppm}$ ammonia at RT.98 Additionally, they synthesized an isostructural $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ MOF featuring Ni centers instead of $\mathtt{C u}$ for the chemiresistive sensors. The $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HITP}\big)_{2}$ coated sensors can reliably detect variable concentrations of ammonia at room temperature. In contrast, the $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ coated sensors displayed no response to ammonia, indicating that the sensing behavior of MOF-coated gas sensors toward specific analytes can be judiciously adjusted via chemical modification of the MOF structure. To further verify this hypothesis, the same team applied three conductive 2D MOFs with similar structures but with varied connectivity for chemiresistive sensing (as shown in Figure 2b).114 The sensors can successfully recognize different types of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) by means of principal component analysis (PCA) (Figure 2c). Each MOF showed distinct responses depending on the type of analyte (Figure 2d). These studies clearly demonstrated that the disparity in the structure of MOFs can cause distinctive responses to different kinds of gases and realized the subtle recognition of specific gases by chemiresistive sensors. In their latest work, the researchers designed and synthesized $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}$ (hexaiminobenzene) $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{I}}_{2}$ $\left(\mathsf{C u}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HIB}\big)_{2}\right)$ and constructed a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemoresistive sensing platform.113 The $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HIB})_{2}$ coated sensors demonstrated excellent ambient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensitivity in the range of $10\mathrm{-}80\%$ relative humidity (RH), superior to those of other known chemiresistive sensors. The authors ascribed the great $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing performance to hydration and charge trapping, which could be illustrated by in situ optical spectroscopy and density functional theory (DFT) calculations. This work significantly promoted the design of chemiresistive sensing with salient sensing performance based on host−guest interactions, and opened up novel perspectives for the potential application of conductive MOFs.
In addition, other research groups have also reported the usage of semiconductive 2D MOFs for chemiresistive sensin g.115,116 For example, Mirica95 et al. synthesized a series of two-dimensional reticular nickel phthalocyanine (NiPc) and nickel naphthalocyanine (NiNPc) based conductive MOFs called NiPc-M $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni},\mathbf{Cu})$ and NiNPc-M, respectively (Figure 2e). Those sensors showed excellent sensitivity in detecting different concentrations of ammonia $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)$ , hydrogen sulfide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}S\right)$ , and nitric oxide (NO). As shown in Figure 2f, the response of NiPc-M MOF-based sensors toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ was faster and higher than that of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ resulting from the efficient charge transfer between the targeted gases captured and the MOF framework. The response saturation was achieved within $10\mathrm{min}$ after exposure to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ at a concentration of $80~\mathrm{ppm}_{;}$ , and the response values of NiPc-Ni and NiPc-Cu were $64\%$ and $98\%$ , respectively. This study offered valuable insights into the development of chemiresistive sensors that rely on semiconductive 2D MOFs. However, achieving lower crosssensitivity and ensuring long-term stability are of paramount importance for practical applications of relevant sensors. In light of this, the same team has reported the use of metallophthalocyanine $\mathrm{'MPc-O_{8}\mathrm{-Cu},}$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}$ or $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ ) MOFs-based sensors for chemiresistive detection of CO.116 The sensors can reliably detect CO up to $0.5{-}3.0~\mathrm{ppm}$ and realized repeatedly multiple detections (Figure 3a). Besides, the resultant sensor could distinguish CO, NO, and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ with parts per million concentrations (Figure 3b). The author indicated that the combination of Cu nodes with CO and amplification of Mpc units contributed to the excellent CO sensing performance. Interestingly, the utilization of bimetallic conductive MOFs is promising for developing novel portable chemiresistive sensors with high sensitivity, low power consumption, and potential remote-control functionality. However, the sensing mechanism of bimetallic 2D MOFs is relatively intricate, and the sensing performance is largely dependent on the crystallinity, morphology, and defect sites of MOFs. The study of chemiresistors based on bimetallic conductive MOFs is still in its infancy, and further exploration is indispensable to facilitate their future industrial application.
It is worth noting that most of the semiconductive MOFs show a powdery crystal state after synthesis, and the random distribution of MOF particles may significantly limit their performance and application in chemiresistive sensing. Researchers have been exploring new preparation approaches for making 2D MOF films with great homogeneity and controllable thickness to enhance their sensing performance such as sensitivity and selectivity.81,99,117,118 Xu et al.99 successfully fabricated thin films of a semiconductive MOF on the hydroxy-functionalized silicon substrates by a spray layer-bylayer liquid-phase epitaxial (LBL-LPE) method. The resultant $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HHTP}\big)_{2}$ thin films with controllable thicknesses exhibited a smooth surface morphology, excellent crystallinity, and high crystallographic orientation. The averaged resistance change of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{\bar{HHT}T P})_{2}$ -coated chemiresistive sensor was estimated to be $129\%$ toward $100\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ which considerably surpassed that of the sensors based on relevant powders or nanorods. This decent work confirmed the important role of the film quality (good control in thickness, grain size, roughness, and orientation) of semiconductive MOF films on the sensing performance of relevant MOF-coated gas sensors. In the meantime, this approach also offered crucial insights into the utilization of MOF thin films with high-quality for devices. In a recent study, Xu’s team uncovered the growth mechanism of MOF films during the preparation process using LBL-LPE method and “in-plane self-limiting and self-repairing” growth mechanism was reported.119 Single ligand-based MOFs failed to meet the various requirements for sensing applications owing to their limited conductivity. With that in mind, Xu and his coworkers recently investigated a novel dual-ligand design strategy to optimize the sensitivity and selectivity of semiconductive MOF-based sensors.115,120 First, variable molar ratios of HHTP and HITP were used to obtain the mixed ligand solutions by adjusting the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ value with ammonium hydroxide. Then, the solution was sprayed onto the substrate to form thin films, which were afterward immersed in a mixture solution of DMF and ethanol to remove the residual ligand (Figure 3c). The sensor based on HITP-doped Cu-HHTP-10C film showed an apparent recovery in response to $100\ \mathrm{\ppm}$ of benzene vapor. The selectivity of HITP-doped Cu-HHTP-10C sensor toward benzene and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gradually heightened with the increase of the doping amount of HITP ligand in the Cu-HHTP framework. Doping Cu-HHTP-10C with $10.0\ \mathrm{\mol}\ \%$ HITP led to an increase of selectivity by about $220\%$ and reversed its sensitivity to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and benzene (Figure 3d). The authors speculated that it might be due to the abundant defects within the film that facilitated the charge transfer between the framework and benzene. In such a fashion, such a sensing behavior can be clearly observed. The study highlighted the obvious advantage in sensing behavior of sensors relying on semiconductive MOFs consisting of mixed ligands. However, the sensing mechanism of the dual-ligand-based MOF is unclear and the selection of doped ligand as well as crystal structure of MOF may be responsible for sensing performance.
In addition, the hydrophilic and hydrophobic properties of MOF films can also be adjusted by surface modification. Dong121 et al. obtained a series of alkylamine, aromatic/aliphatic hydrocarbon, or octadecyltrimethoxysilane (OTMS) modified phthalocyanine-based 2D $\mathrm{{Ni_{2}[M P c(N H)_{8}]}}$ MOF films with tunable thicknesses (70 to ${500}\mathrm{nm},$ ) by chemical grafting. The recovery speed of the $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{[CuPc(NH)_{8}]}$ -OTMS film increased dramatically during humidity sensing (from 50 to $10~\mathrm{s}$ ) (Figure 3e). The observation was ascribed to the hydrophobic modification of the MOF film surface by the OTMS alkyl chains, which restrained the diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ into the MOF backbone. The $\mathrm{{Ni_{2}[C u P c(N H)_{8}]}}$ -OTMS coated sensors manifested remarkably quicker response $\left(36~\mathsf{s}\right)$ and recovery time (13 s) (Figure 3f) toward $400\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of methanol with a detection limit of $10\mathrm{ppm}$ compared with the pristine one. They claimed that the sensing performance was superior to the reported chemiresistive sensors at room temperature based on other sensing materials. This work illustrated the selective detection of chemiresistive sensors with fast response/recovery, and opened up new possibilities for the development of chemiresistors with excellent sensing performance based on surface-modified 2D MOF films. More recently, $\mathtt{X u}$ et al.122 successfully prepared 3D $\mathtt{C u}$ -HHTP films on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanowire arrays in a controlled layer-by-layer manner (Figure 4a), which exhibited a larger surface area and shorter charge/mass transport path. As shown in Figure 4b, the chemiresistive sensors based on 3D Cu-HHTP films showed a $250\%$ increase in response to 1 ppm of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and a 1000-fold decrease in detection limit $(5\mathrm{ppb})$ compared with that of the sensors based on 2D films, stemming from the better gas enrichment capability of this unique 3D structure, as speculated by the authors. This study provided a novel method for the fabrication of gas sensors with precise control of heterostructured MOF thin films featuring increased porosity and charge transport.
MOF-on-MOF Heterogeneous Structure Coated Chemiresistors. Additionally, heterogeneous structures were designed by stacking different MOFs. So far, various strategies for synthesizing heterogeneous structures have been reported, including epitaxial growth,123−126 controllable self-assembly,127 and interfacial engineering.128,129 However, lattice mismatch between the chosen MOFs often hinders the construction of MOF-on-MOF heterostructures. To overcome this issue, Xu et al.31 utilized van der Waals forces to connect $\mathtt{C u}$ -HHTP and CuTCPP films. They first fabricated Cu-HHTP thin films by the layer-by-layer spray method, and then the Cu-TCPP layer was integrated onto the surface of the Cu-HHTP thin films, affording the Cu-HHTP/Cu-TCPP heterogeneous structure (Figure 4c) targeted for gas sensing. The Cu-HHTP layer was utilized as the sensing material, whereas the Cu-TCPP acted as the molecular sieving layer. The Cu-TCPP can selectively capture ammonia molecules whereas allowed the pass-through of benzene, leading to the successful detection toward benzene with enhanced selectivity. Hence, the Cu-TCPP/Cu-HHTP coated sensing devices showed dramatically improved selectivity toward benzene over $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ compared to that of the sensor based on the Cu-HHTP film (Figure 4d), and its response was enhanced as the concentration of benzene increased (Figure 4e). Impressively, the highly oriented MOF-on-MOF thin film obtained by the “modular assembly” stamping approach alleviated the lattice matching requirement of the earlier reports regarding the fabrication of MOF-on-MOF films. This study demonstrated the impact of heterostructured MOFs with excellent sensing properties targeted for chemiresistive sensing, and laid the foundation for the subsequent development of heterostructured MOF-based chemiresistive sensors. Nevertheless, the preparation of MOF-on-MOF structures often involves multiple steps and requires careful control over experimental conditions, which is certainly time-consuming and brings in complexity. These limitations need to be well addressed to fully realize their potential for practical applications.
MOF Composite Coated Chemiresistors. Apart from the mere usage of semiconductive MOFs/MOF-on-MOF heterogeneous structures for chemiresistive sensing, MOFs composited with other (semi)conductive materials such as semiconducting metal oxides,130−133 carbon-based nanomaterials,134 and/or encapsulated with semiconducting molecules can be used for chemiresistive gas sensing as well. For example, Bandosz135 et al. investigated the usage of MOF/graphene based hybrid materials in chemiresistive sensors to detect variable concentrations of ammonia. The HKUST- $.(\varpi\mathrm{GO}$ based sensors were capable of detecting variable concentrations of ammonia within $100{-}500~\mathrm{\ppm}$ at room temperature. However, the response achieved by this approach is not as high as that of other functionalized graphenes, which may be due to the crystal collapse of the MOF pores. By contrast, the structure of the MOF can be effectively maintained by loading the conducting monomer molecules within the pore of the MOF and subsequently adding an initiator to facilitate polymerization. Uemura136 et al. successfully realized the loading of 3,4- ethylenedioxythiophene (EDOT) within the cavities of MIL$101{\big(}\mathrm{Cr}{\big)}$ , and underwent oxidative polymerization by iodine vapors to achieve the polymerization of EDOT within the cavities of $\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ and to improve its conductivity. The conductivity of the MIL-101( $\mathrm{(cr)}$ /PEDOT composite $(10^{-3}\:S$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1},$ was several orders of magnitude higher than that of the pristine MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ $\left(10^{-11}\thinspace\mathrm{S~cm^{-1}}\right)$ . Additionally, the specific surface area of the composite could maintain $803\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , and the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ /PEDOT coated sensors effectively enhanced the sensitivity toward $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ compared to the pure PEDOT-based sensors. Encapsulating semiconductive monomers into the pores/channels of MOFs and then realizing their polymerization can endow MOFs with both electrical conductivity and compromised porosity. More recently, Calame137 et al. reported the usage of a semiconductive $\mathtt{C u}$ -HHTP-TCNQ hybrid films in gas sensing. The hybrid films were composed of filamentous and cubic crystals, and the mean conductivity of Cu-HHTP-TCNQbased chemiresistive sensors was $0.0\dot{3}3\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The work justified the hybridization strategy of an exotic phase with MOFs for chemiresistive sensors.
## MOF-BASED CAPACITIVE GAS SENSORS
Capacitive gas sensors feature simplicity in structure, miniaturization, and facile operation at room temperature, effectively solving the problem of high-power consumption. The mechanism of capacitive sensors relies on the change of dielectric constant138 after adsorbing the target molecules. The capacitor is composed of electrodes separated by an insulating electrolyte (Figure 5a,b).139 The capacitance of the primary capacitor can be expressed as follows:
$$
C=\varepsilon_{r}{\frac{\varepsilon_{0}A}{d}}
$$
where $\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_{r}$ is the relative static permittivity of dielectric materials, $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{0}}$ denotes the permittivity of free space; $d$ presents the distance between the two electrodes; and $A$ is the area of the plate. The $\ensuremath{\varepsilon}_{r}$ would change after adsorbing diverse gases, and then it can be detected in the form of capacitance/impedance fluctuations, which are related to gas type and concentration. The earlier capacitive sensor based on MOFs was reported by Achmann and co-workers,139 and several MOFs (Al-BDC, Fe-BTC, and CuBTC) have been used as sensing materials.
Based on the sensing mechanism of changing the permittivity of MOF films, Omran140 et al. for the first time, applied a $\mathrm{{Cu}(B D C){\cdot}{x}H_{2}O}$ thin-film coated IDEs to capacitive sensors for detecting VOCs. At higher concentrations, the capacitance of the $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{{BDC}}){\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ coated sensor changed linearly toward variable concentrations of acetone, ethanol, and methanol. Moreover, the MOF-coated sensor also exhibited a linear response toward toluene at 125 to $500~\mathrm{ppm}$ . Salama and coworkers successfully synthesized fumarate-based face-centered cubic (fcu)-MOF141 and NDC-Y-fcu-MOF142 thin films. The latter showed high sensitivity in detecting $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ and the detection limit was deduced to be about $100\ \mathrm{ppb}_{i}$ , even with the interference of moisture or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The NDC-Y-fcu-MOF coated sensors with good stability, showed the potential for practical application. However, these sensors all showed certain cross sensitivities to interference gases and the response was relatively low.
Regarding this, several groups have investigated the functionalization of MOFs to improve the selectivity and response performance of a MOF-coated capacitive sensor. Zhao et al.10 integrated $\mathrm{Mg-MOF-74}$ films with controllable morphologies onto silicon substrates by the in situ solvothermal growth approach under an appropriate metal to ligand ratio, and modified the pore environment of Mg-MOF-74 with ethylenediamine. The $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ -MOF-74 film coated sensing devices exhibited a linear response toward benzene vapor $\left(2\mathrm{-}100\ \mathrm{ppm}\right)$ at room temperature and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the range of $200{-}5000$ ppm. Interestingly, the response of the sensor after the modification of ethylenediamine to benzene vapor decreased by about $60\%$ ; where the response to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ increased by about $25\%$ (Figure 5c). The authors believed that the observed reduction in sensitivity to benzene originated from the decrease in the porosity of the Mg-MOF-74 framework caused by amine coordination and weak Lewis acid−base interactions between the active sites of MOFs and the $\pi$ -cloud entities of gas analytes (Figure 6d). In contrast, the sensitivity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was enhanced due to the specific interaction between the amine groups and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 5d). This study demonstrates that it is promising to develop MOF-coated sensors with potential mass production and commercialization by regulating the MOF-analyte interaction. Recently, the same team utilized NUS-8 nanosheets with different functional groups $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2},-\mathrm{CH}_{3},-\mathrm{H}\right)$ and fabricated corresponding capacitive sensors.72 The above sensors delivered different sensing characteristics to acetone in the parts per million range, and the NUS- $\mathsf{B}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ coated device showed the highest sensitivity to acetone. The excellent sensitivity was attributed to the stronger interaction between $\mathrm{NUS}{\cdot}8{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and acetone. The significance of this study lies in its potential to integrate MOFs onto miniaturized devices with potential mass production, which could pave the way for the commercial application of MOF-coated sensing devices.
Apart from the direct usage of MOFs as active layers of capacitive sensors, the synthesis and application of MOF-based composite were also reported.30 Quite recently, Smet et al.101 dispersed nanoparticles of $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}.53\mathrm{(Al)}$ into a Matrimid polyimide to gain MOF-based composite films. The addition of MOF to the polymer resulted in a decrease in the total capacitance of capacitive devices, which can be explained by the low dielectric constant of MOF particles. The MOF/Matrimidbased sensors showed a more pronounced response to methanol. The study indicated that MOF-based composites can further enhance the sensitivity and selectivity of capacitors. However, issues such as slow response speed, high cost, and complex material structure hinder its practical application. To address the above problems, Huang et al.143 reported a novel capacitive sensor consisting of an ionic conducting MOF (ICMOF) film. The IC-MOF coated capacitive sensors can detect ultralow concentrations of dimethyl carbonate (DMC) (50 ppb) with response times as low as 2s, which enabled an efficient and rapid response to electrolyte leaks. The ionic conductivity of IC-MOF has been demonstrated to be critical, and the carrier $\left(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\right)$ -electrolyte vapor interaction is the key factor in achieving high sensitivity. This work demonstrated the advantage of ICMOFs as a capacitive sensing material, paving the way for its portable applications. Similarly, Fernandez et al.30 fabricated ionic liquids (IL)/MOF composite based on a strategy of endowing MOFs with ionic conductivity (Figure 5e). The IL/ MOF-based capacitive sensors witnessed fast changes (less than a second) in capacitance (from 0.05 to ${7}\mathrm{pF}.$ ) dependent on the type of target gases (Figure 5f). This research realized the reliable combination between MOF integration and spray printing techniques, and may point out new directions for the further exploration of MOF-based capacitive sensors.
## MOF-COATED FETS AND/OR KELVIN PROBES FOR GAS SENSING
Field-effect transistors (FETs) have the advantages of high sensitivity, facile device configuration, low energy consumption, and good practicability.144 The classical FETs are made up of a source electrode, leakage electrode, gate electrode, semiconductor material channel, and insulating gate oxide layer (Figure 6a,b). The channel current of the semiconductor material between the source and the drain pole can be modulated by the gate end. The operating principle of FETs is usually based on the variation of work function after surface functionalization and/or uptake of gases, in terms of the contact potential difference $(\mathrm{CPD},\Delta\varphi)$ between the fixed electrode and the reference one in the Kelvin probe configuration (Figure 6b).97,100,145 Several groups have studied the detection of multifunctional gas sensors based on the Kelvin probe method. For example, Davydovskaya et al.146 first investigated the usage of $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC MOF for gas sensing with work function readout. Hexanal $\left(1\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ and toluene $({\boldsymbol{5}}{\mathrm{ppm}})$ scarcely caused changes in work function, but an obvious and reversible reaction was observed when exposed to pentanal $\left(1\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ , suggesting that the gas response was independent of the chemical properties but was mainly affected by the size matching between the pore of the MOF and gas molecules. The team also studied the effect of relative humidity on the Cu-BTC-based sensors, and relevant sensors showed response under exposure to diverse aldehydes (ethanal, propanal, pentanal, and hexanal) at lower concentrations.147 Minor responses had been observed for ethanol, propanal, and hexanol compared to pentanal in parts per million concentrations due to their disparity in polarity. The aforementioned studies have reported the utilization of MOFs in FET devices, serving as a valuable reference for subsequent research endeavors. Stassen et al.100 presented the fabrication of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -based FET sensors, whose CPD shift was several tens of millivolts when exposed to different dimethyl methyl phosphonate (DMMP) concentrations in a Kelvin probe mode. The lowest concentration detected was around $3\mathrm{\ppm}$ (Figure
6c,d). The study indicated that electrically insulating MOFs can also be applied in FETs for gas sensing.
Simultaneously, dozens of 2D layered MOFs with high conductivities have also been applied to FETs. $\mathrm{Xu}^{148}$ and coworkers applied $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ to FETs. The team used a selfassembly process to prepare ultrasmooth and compact $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ films using an air/liquid interface. The $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HI}\bar{\mathrm{T}}\mathrm{P})_{2}$ coated FET presents a typical p-type transistor with a current switching ratio of about $2\times10,^{3}$ and the hole mobility was approximately $48.6~\mathrm{cm}^{2}~\mathrm{V}^{-1}~\mathrm{s}^{-1},$ which surpassed most of the organic semiconductors. This phenomenon was attributed to the formation of a fully extended charged-delocalized layer in the lattice plane between the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ centers and the HITP linkers, facilitating carrier transport. The present study has successfully reported the fabrication of field-effect transistors that exhibit remarkable switching ratios and noteworthy carrier mobility. These outcomes underscore the vast potential of two-dimensional conductive MOFs in the realm of electronic devices, and significantly advance the usage of MOFs in the field of microelectronics. Similarly, Sayyad149 obtained $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HHTP}\big)_{2}$ nanorods by solvothermal synthesis and fabricated FET based gas sensors. Note that the material used here is actually Ni-CAT1, which consists of two distinct types of alternatively stacked layers. One layer is composed of a 2D framework of Ni atoms combined with two deprotonated HHTP linkers and two water ligands, and the other comprises discrete complexes of Ni atoms coordinated to one HHTP linker and to four water ligands. Relevant studies about Ni-CAT-1 have been reported by $\mathrm{Yaghi},^{42}$ Dinca,38 and Deng.40 The structures of $\bar{\bf N i_{3}}(\bar{\bf H H T P})_{2}^{\bar{\bf\Phi}}$ and Ni-CAT-1 are summarized in Table 1. In order not to mislead the reader, we will use the correct formula information Ni-CAT-1 for the rest of the description of this work). The NiCAT-1 coated FET sensors exhibited a high sensitivity and excellent selectivity toward $S O_{2}$ . The current of the leakage electrode changed distinctly in the presence of $S O_{2}$ with a detection limit of $625\ \mathrm{ppb}$ . Zhou150 et al. synthesized highly oriented $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HITP}\big)_{2}$ nanofiber arrays (NFAs) via the liquid phase epitaxial growth. The carrier mobility of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\big(\mathrm{HITP}\big)_{2}$ NFAs coated FETs was up to $5.09\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{\dot{s}}^{-1}$ and the on/off ratio was around $9.6\times10^{^{-}3}$ and the sensors exhibited excellent response and recovery in detecting VOCs. This study demonstrated a novel strategy to prepare highly oriented MOF NFAs and provided new inspiration for the preparation of 2D MOF-coated FET devices. All of these aforementioned studies have significantly contributed to the advancement of FET sensing devices based on 2D (semi)conducting MOFs.
The usage of MOF composites as active layers represents an important research direction of MOF-coated FET sensors in recent years.151,152 Shirsat153 synthesized the composite of $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(B T C)_{2}/O H}$ -functionalized single walled carbon nanotubes (OH-SWNTs) through a solvothermal method. The conductive pathway generated by the incorporation of OH-SWNTs into $\bar{\bf N i}_{3}(\bar{\bf B}\bar{\bf T}C\bar{\bf)}_{2}$ could promote charge transport. The $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}/$ OH-SWNTs composite showed $p$ -type behavior, which can be confirmed by the FET measurement. The sensor based on $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}/\mathrm{OH}$ -SWNTs revealed excellent sensing characteristics toward variable concentrations of ${\mathrm{~SO}}_{2},$ attributed to the vacancies resulting from the interaction between $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the composites. The $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}/\mathrm{OH}$ -SWNTs-based sensor showed good sensitivity toward $S O_{2}$ than that of other gases $(\mathrm{NO}_{2},\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},$ and CO) and the nucleophilic nature of those gas molecules was responsible for their inferior performance. The study suggests that combining MOF with carbon nanotubes holds promise for various applications in FETs. Furthermore, the judicious combination of MOFs and carbon nanofibers presents an appealing avenue for future exploration. Park and coworkers fabricated a series of FET devices based on poly(3- hexylthiophene) (P3TH) and ZIF-8,151 carbon nanofibers (CNFs) and ZIF-67.154 CNF and ZIF-67 nanoparticles were combined to form ZIF-67@CNF nanofibers embedded in the P3TH matrix. The hole mobility of hybrid fiber $(1.9\times10^{-3}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ $\mathrm{~V~}^{-1}\textbf{s}^{-1})$ decreased compared with that of the original P3TH device $\left(6.0\times10^{-3}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\hat{\mathrm{V}}^{-1}s^{-1}\right)$ on account of the adhesion of insulating ZIF-67 particles to CNF, which hindered the charge transfer. The sensitivity of the sensor based on ZI $\mathsf{i}.67@\mathrm{CNF}/$ P3TH toward $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ enhanced markedly in comparison with that of the ZIF-67/P3TH-coated sensing devices (Figure $^{7\mathrm{a},\mathrm{b}}$ ) since the homogeneous distribution of ZIF-67 particles on CNFs in P3TH with a smaller particle size and higher specific surface area (Figure $\mathrm{7c}$ ). This work contributed to the development of FET devices coated with inorganic-MOF hybrid materials. Although the integration of inorganic materials with MOFs represents an effective approach to improve the surface sensitivity, selfaggregating of MOF particles may occur during the blending process.155 This drawback requires a good solution before they can be put into practice in a variety of sensing situations. Recently, Zhu et al.156 r eported the fabrication of FET sensors based on $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}/\mathbf{MOF}$ heterojunctions to detect $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (Figure 7d). The electrical properties of FDM-23 can be improved by modifying the structure of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ . During the epitaxial growth of FDM-23, its lattice did not perfectly match that of $\mathrm{MoS}_{2},$ which resulted in reduced carrier mobility but improved gas sensing behavior. The $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}/\mathbf{MOF}$ -based FETs revealed a response of $3,303\%$ toward $100\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ at a $-10\mathrm{V}$ gate voltage (Figure 7e), which was 22.475-times higher than that of the bare $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}.$ - based FETs. The authors ascribed the excellent response performance to the n-type doping and porous structures obtained by modification. This work offers a feasible strategy to fabricate MOF coated FET sensors with high sensitivity.
## MOF-BASED ELECTROCHEMICAL GAS SENSORS
The operating mechanism of electrochemical devices for gas sensing leans on the reaction between the sensing electrodes and the target gases.157 These kinds of sensors exhibit good linearity to the gas concentration, excellent measurement accuracy, and repeatability. The typical electrochemical gas sensors are composed of a sensing electrode and a counter electrode, which are separated by a thin electrolytic layer. However, the stability and therefore lifetime of those sensors are always problematic due to the continuous electrochemical reactions on the electrode surfaces. The insulating behavior of MOFs resists inevitable charge transfer in the electrochemical process, which is not favorable. Therefore, developing new sensing electrode (SE) materials is a vital research direction for MOF-based electrochemical sensors. A few studies have described the synthesis of new SE materials. For instance, Lu and coworkers158 developed and manufactured a new type of mixedpotential type solid-state electrolyte (MPSE) utilized for gas sensing (Figure 8a). A series of Ni/Fe-MOF composites were obtained by adjusting the mass ratio of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Two different dependencies between the response values of the Ni-MOF coated sensors and the NO concentration were observed. The sensor showed a linear response to variable concentrations of NO concentration in the low range; where exhibited a logarithm response in the high concentration range. This was caused by the diffusion and electrochemical reaction limitation according to the paper. In the meanwhile, the author associated the excellent sensing performance intimately with the lowest oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) activity and the highest NO electrocatalytic activity, which were justified by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS)
analyses, and cyclic voltammetry (CV). The sensitivity of the sensors was $-19\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{ppm}^{-1}$ (Figure 8b) and the detection limit was derived to be 20 ppb (Figure 8c). In addition, those sensors showed excellent selectivity toward NO other than ethanol, acetone, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (Figure 8c). This work has tremendously facilitated the development of room-temperature electrochemical sensors.
More attention has also been focused on MOF/graphene oxide (MOF/GO) composites to modify the working electrodes. He et al.160 reported a PCN-222/GO composite and fabricated the electrochemical sensors. The PCN-222/GO coated sensors showed good repeatability and outstanding selectivity. Recently, Wang et al.159 further advanced the application of MOF/GO composites in electrochemical sensors. They first fabricated $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{INA})_{2}/\mathrm{GO}$ by the hydrothermal method mixed $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{INA})_{2}$ and HINA in ethanol/DDW/GO solution by ultrasonication and then realized the formation of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{INA})_{2}\mathrm{'}G\mathrm{O}$ by the hydrothermal method. The composite was afterward coated on IDEs and then $\mathrm{Cu}\big(\mathrm{INA}\big)_{2}/\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{IDE}$ was achieved by electrochemical reduction. The CV curves scarcely changed with the increase of the circle number throughout the scan on $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{INA})_{2}/\mathrm{IDE}$ compared with $\mathrm{Cu}\big(\mathrm{INA}\big)_{2}/\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{IDE}$ or rGO/IDE. The redox signal of GO/ IDE was significantly smaller than that of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{INA})_{2}/\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{IDE}$ (Figure 8d) because of the lower electronic mobility. The sensitivity of the $\mathrm{Cu(INA)_{2}/r G O/I D E}$ sensor surpassed other $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ sensors at higher $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ concentrations (Figure 8e). The response of the $\mathrm{Cu(INA)}_{2}/\mathrm{rGO/IDE}$ based electrochemical sensor toward $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ remained linear with the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ concentration (Figure 9f). The authors concluded that there were two reasons accounting for this phenomenon. On the one hand, the large surface area of $\mathrm{Cu}\big(\mathrm{INA}\big)_{2}$ provided more activation sites, promoting the adsorption and diffusion of gas molecules. On the other hand, the $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{INA})_{2}$ and GO can synergistically create a mechanism for carrier migration. This study enlarged the possible applications of MOF composites in electrochemical sensors.
## QCM-BASED MOF SENSORS
Quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) sensors consist of a thin slice of quartz crystal with electrodes deposited on both front and back sides, showing low cost, simple setup, stable property, and high sensitivity.161 Its underlying principle relies on the mass change of the active layer after gas capture, which affects the resonance frequencies or acoustic frequencies of the quartz crystal surfaces (Figure 9a). As such, the frequency change of active layer can be monitored in real time to realize gas sensin g.33,97 The frequency shift (Δf) can be gained according to the Sauerbrey’s equation:162
$$
\Delta f=-\frac{2f_{0}^{2}}{A\sqrt{\mu\rho}}\Delta m
$$
where $\Delta f$ represents the frequency shift generated by the variation of the adsorption of gas molecules $(\breve{\Delta}m).$ , A represents a constant that depends on the property of the crystal used; $f_{o}$ is the original resonance frequency; $\mu$ and $\rho$ are the shear modulus and density, respectively. The $\Delta f$ and performance parameters can be correlated to the targeted gas concentration. The MOFs widely used in QCM sensors for the detection of various gases/ vapors mainly include HKUST-1,163 UiO-66,164 and ZIF-8.165 Devkota et al.165 used the nanoporous ZIF-8 film as the sensing layer in QCM sensors to detect $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The selectivity of the ZIF-8 coated QCM sensors toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is significantly superior to that of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ because the adsorption effect of ZIF-8 on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was better than that of $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ as well as the larger molecular weight of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Chappanda and co-workers166 deposited ZIF-8 films with uniform thickness on QCM surfaces for the detection of acetone. The ZIF-8-based QCM sensors had long-term stability but slightly inferior selectivity to acetone. The authors envisioned that the sensing characteristics and stability of sensing devices were dependent on the sensing materials. Xu and co-workers167 applied ZIF-90 onto QCM-based sensors to detect acetone, which showed excellent selectivity toward acetone than other target gases (Figure 9b). The response and recovery time of the QCM sensor based on the ZIF-90 were only about 12 and $^{17\ s,}$ respectively. The sensors displayed high acetone sensitivity with a low detection limit of 20 ppb and a high frequency response value of $10.2~\mathrm{\Hz}$ . (Figure 9c), compared with that of ZIF-8-based QCM sensors. This desirable sensing property was ascribed to the stable and strong adsorption affinity between the aldehyde groups decorated within the framework of ZIF-90 and the acetone molecules. In addition, the team explored the influence of environmental humidity on the sensing properties of the ZIF-90-based QCM sensor, where the response increased instantaneously as the humidity increased. Several other groups further explored the detection characteristics of QCM sensors toward humidity. Eddaoudi et al.11 prepared CNT/HKUST-1 composite thin films with thicknesses between 2 to $3\mu\mathrm{m}$ on QCM electrodes by spin-coating. The CNT/HKUST-1 coated sensors exhibited much better sensitivity $[-141\mathrm{Hz}$ per RH) to relative humidity compared to that of the pristine sensors based on pure HKUST1 film (Figure 9d). This study showed significant value in enhancing the sensitivity of MOF-coated sensors. Several other research groups have explored the potential of QCM sensors based on different types of MOFs to detect a variety of gases, including MIL-101,168,169 KAUST-7/8,170 and Cu(DDS)2(Cl)2(MeOH)2.171
In addition, sensor-array-based QCM has also been reported to improve cross-sensitivities. Recently, Qin et al.172 fabricated a QCM sensor array made up of six QCM sensors to detect ternary xylene isomer mixtures (Figure 9e). Each sensor array manifested a detection limit as low as 1 ppm for each pure gas analyte and showed different sensitivities toward the same analyte (Figure 9f), resulting from the differentiated characteristics of the various MOFs as well as the difference in thickness of the MOF films. The authors ascribed this behavior to their different structures and adsorption sites (Figure $9\mathrm{g}$ ). In addition, the same team also successfully applied the sensor array in identifying binary and ternary xylene mixtures. The average accuracy in discrimination was $96.5\%$ for distinguishing the gas mixture composed of 16 different isomer vapors. This study showed the intriguing application of MOF-sensor arrays for distinguishing the VOC isomers.
## CONCLUSION AND PERSPECTIVES
MOFs have drawn extensive attention in the field of gas sensors because of their unique features. Great advances have been achieved in fabricating MOF-coated gas sensors with intriguing sensing performances. This review reviews the latest progress of MOF-coated electronic devices for gas sensing relying on diverse transduction mechanisms such as chemiresistive, capacitive, electrochemical, FET-based, and mass fluctuation. The structural characteristics were associated with the sensing behaviors of relevant MOF-coated sensors. Although plenty of MOF-coated gas sensors have achieved excellent sensing properties, their large-scale production and commercialization remain extremely challenging. More efforts are badly needed to address some issues, as shown below. First, the design strategy of novel 2D MOFs with $\pi$ -conjugated structure and even other topologies with high stability, considerable charge delocalization, and specific interaction with targeted analytes needs to be developed. Other promising strategies to enhance the conductivity of MOFs would be effective supplements, for instance, doping of MOFs with (semi)conductive agents or materials. Second, technologies for precise integration and/or patterning of MOFs onto miniaturized gas sensors with good homogeneity, controllable thickness, and preferential orientation have yet to be developed. Apart from the usage of modern microfabrication technologies, including thin film deposition and photolithography, developing MOFs with good solution processability would be a solution to achieve this. Third, sensor arrays and heterostructures composed of different phases need to be constructed to substantially increase the detection selectivity and lower the detection limit. Forth, the stability and repeatability of MOF sensors need to be well justified, which can be alleviated by using robust bonds bridged by multidentate ligands and highly valent metal centers. Fifth, the effective usage of machine learning/function-oriented algorithms might bring in new insights into data processing and structure−property elucidation toward the MOF-based sensors. Obviously, tremendous efforts are still needed to solve the aforementioned issues before their practical applications in various sensing scenarios.
